    "knowledge_point": "Finding a Cubic Polynomial Given Three Real Roots and a Leading Coefficient",
    "problem": "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-2,{x}_{3}=5,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $2.$ Find $P(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-2$ and ${x}_{3}=5,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =2(x-(-2))(x-(-2))(x-5) \\\\  & =2(x+2)(x+2)(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)(x+2)(x-5) & =2({x}^{2}+4x+4)(x-5) \\\\  & =2({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2}+4x-20x-20) \\\\  & =2({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-16x-20) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-32x-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-32x-40.$",
