    "reasoning": "All 16 selected answers are mathematically correct and supported by their own captured worked solutions. q-120893: writing a=arccos(7/8), the roots are a and 2π−a; their product is approximately 2.9199, rounding to 2.9. q-120169: a radius-3 circle meets the negative x-axis at (−3,0). q-107538: 2(x+2)^2(x−5)=2x^3−2x^2−32x−40. q-18621: the coefficient is binom(13,11)·3^2=78·9=702. q-135896: the full expansion is 256−256x+96x^2−16x^3+x^4, matching the selected initial terms. q-14050: (x−3)^2=4(y+4), so y=0 gives x=7,−1. q-14241: (y−4)^2=−2(x−2), so x=0 gives y=2,6. q-31951: the chain rule gives 12/(|6x−1|sqrt((6x−1)^2−1)), equivalent to the selected simplified derivative. q-107070: both denominator factors cancel, leaving x^2−x+1 with removable holes at −1 and 5, hence no vertical asymptotes. q-114027 and q-114023: substitutions u=sqrt(3)x and u=6x give inverse-sine coefficients 8/sqrt(3)=8sqrt(3)/3 and 1/36. q-2507 and q-49551: substitutions u=5x and u=x/8 give inverse-tangent coefficients 1/5 and 1/4; the latter prefactor is equivalent to the selected quotient. q-114051 and q-114052: differentiating arcsec(sqrt(k)x) gives 1/(|x|sqrt(kx^2−1)) for k=5 and 2. q-19393: the integral is 5[arcsin(x/4)] from 0 to 2, giving 5π/6. Authored exposition errors are preserved in the supplied source: q-18621 and q-135896 write ordinary fractions where binomial coefficients are required, and q-107070's statement that the denominator is never zero applies only to the simplified expression, not the original denominator. These defects do not change any stated final answer.",
